{
  "dom_id": "step-q181987",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={1,2}\\times (-1,1)$ in the Cartesian plane?",
  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={1,2}\\times (-1,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $1$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {1,2},\\,y\\in (-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}\u2282S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (-1,1)}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/11522acdfeaad022847fedbcf6052424216587f99f2691e80ed96152f30818a4.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}\u2282S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/90b6a9c00d366e4a03c8c974476fdf06bc9fa73c9c26e9a0cd6ba9a4bda8e66d.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/bbf0d5ecc57bc30c7a3f4db7a1d30b27633e4586b2768e315715df72ce41333f.png)",
